-- title:
--   A set of uniform positive density at every scale is co-null
-- statement:
--   If a measurable $U \subseteq \mathbb{R}^d$ satisfies, for **some** $c_0 > 0$ and **every** centre $y$ and
--   **every** radius $r > 0$,
--
--   $$c_0 r^d \;\le\; \lvert U \cap \bar B(y,r)\rvert,$$
--
--   then $\lvert U^{\mathsf c}\rvert = 0$.
--
--   By the Lebesgue density theorem almost every point of $U^{\mathsf c}$ would otherwise be a density point of
--   the complement, contradicting the uniform lower bound at small radii.
--
--   **This marks the exact reach of the visible-dense method.** `QFS.VisibleDense` asks precisely this
--   condition of the set where a cone direction is realised. So that hypothesis is not a mild
--   "some directions are common" assumption — it forces the direction to be realised almost everywhere. The
--   locally-dominated hypotheses exist to weaken it.
--
--   Part of the beyond-the-paper work attacking this mission's open frontier, `QFS.sobolevInclusion`. Nothing here appears in Bux–Kassmann–Schulze.
